  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 215 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nWarm Thursday and Friday: Confidence is high that above average\ntemps will occur as the flow aloft becomes more \nsoutherly/southwesterly. This is due to a larger upper level \nsystem moving from the West Coast into the Rockies. Latest NBM \ncontinues to support highs in the upper 60s to around 70 both \ndays. The lack of lift and moisture will keep rain chances near \nzero. These high temps will be roughly 10-15 degrees above \naverage. \n\nRain Chances This Weekend: Ensembles continue to suggest that\nthe upper level energy will slide east into Colorado and\neventually Missouri this weekend. This energy and a front will\ncertainly provide for a lifting mechanism for rain however the \nmain limiting factor for rainfall appears to be moisture \nquality and depth. Latest NBM only has a 20-40% chance of \nrainfall amounts of 0.10in or greater with this system. The \nprogressive nature of the front is another limiting factor for \nhigher amounts. Currently very low forecast mean MU CAPE values \nare keeping thunderstorm chances less than 10 percent.\n\nThe overall system continues to slow as well with an increasing\npotential that most of Saturday is warm and dry with an evening\nor overnight frontal passage that provides the rain chances. \nCooler air aloft then looks to build in for Sunday into early \nnext week with temps closer to or slightly below average.",
